WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:00.130 --> 00:04.200
So once you've developed your initial issue tree,
how can you test it and improve it?

2
00:04.200 --> 00:09.000
One tool that we can use to help test or improve upon our issue tree
is something we call MECE.

3
00:09.000 --> 00:12.260
So what is MECE? Mutually exclusive, collectively exhaustive.

4
00:12.580 --> 00:16.510
No overlaps, no gaps. When we think about mutually exclusive,

5
00:16.840 --> 00:22.630
we want to make sure there's no overlap in the issues —
so if there's an issue, it only appears in one branch of that tree.

6
00:23.140 --> 00:29.920
When we think about collectively exhaustive, this is making sure there are no gaps —
so it's making sure that, at the right-hand side of the tree,

7
00:30.310 --> 00:35.200
all of the issues that we've identified are fully captured there,
and we've effectively broadened the scope of our solutions.

8
00:35.200 --> 00:39.400
So let's use an example of what is MECE and what is not MECE.

9
00:39.400 --> 00:43.620
So if I think about what is MECE — if I'm defining my problem to be:

10
00:44.260 --> 00:50.400
how do I break down profitability? I may decompose my tree
into the revenue drivers and the cost drivers.

11
00:50.400 --> 00:54.400
Let me give you some examples of what is not MECE, in terms of a list.

12
00:54.400 --> 00:58.720
So if my list included breakfast, lunch and meals —
well, that wouldn't be MECE, because

13
00:58.790 --> 01:05.150
breakfast and lunch are part of meals. Therefore it's not mutually exclusive,
because there's an overlap in the two different issues.

14
01:05.930 --> 01:11.400
If I was explaining how a traffic light worked to my son
and I said there are green lights and red lights —

15
01:11.400 --> 01:16.400
well, I've not been collectively exhaustive,
because I haven't mentioned the yellow lights.

16
01:16.400 --> 01:20.600
So there are a couple of tips on how best to get your issue tree to be MECE.

17
01:20.600 --> 01:25.020
Number one: think about how you can get as much input as possible,
particularly from experts — and the collective wisdom

18
01:25.090 --> 01:30.240
will oftentimes lead to a superior answer
versus what one person can come up with themselves.

19
01:30.570 --> 01:35.230
And with that, it's an exercise that really benefits from an iterative process.

20
01:35.590 --> 01:39.680
Don't think that you need to get the exact right answer out of the gate.
Better to get an

21
01:40.010 --> 01:46.600
answer that's 80% of the way there, and then test and iterate that as you go —
rather than over-invest or spend too much time up front

22
01:46.600 --> 01:49.400
trying to get to the perfect solution.

23
01:49.400 --> 01:53.350
So once you've got your tree as close to MECE as you think you can,
the next step is prioritisation.

24
01:53.700 --> 01:58.200
And this is where it's really important to think about the sequencing.

25
01:58.200 --> 02:04.030
And one of the traps people sometimes fall into
is eliminating items on the issue tree too early.

26
02:04.360 --> 02:08.600
The best way to do it is to have a more collectively exhaustive issue tree,

27
02:08.600 --> 02:13.200
so you have all the issues laid out in front of you,
and then to prioritise —

28
02:13.200 --> 02:18.600
and think about not only which issues you want to tackle first,
but what branches of the tree may not apply.

29
02:18.600 --> 02:20.220
So, prioritising too early

30
02:20.490 --> 02:25.400
sometimes links back to the definition phase of the problem statement,
where you're thinking about the scope of the solution set.

31
02:25.400 --> 02:29.540
And so if the problem you're trying to solve is:

32
02:29.890 --> 02:37.700
how do we grow revenue? You might think about what we can do in terms of price,
or what we can do in terms of volume, right — selling more widgets, if you will.

33
02:38.050 --> 02:44.400
But another way to drive revenue growth might be through acquisition, right?
Acquiring another company and basically combining the revenue of the two entities.

34
02:44.400 --> 02:50.200
It may be the case that acquisitions are off the table
because of budget constraints or others,

35
02:50.200 --> 02:55.100
but if you don't include that as an option in terms of growing revenue,

36
02:55.210 --> 03:00.770
you may too early, and too narrowly, define the scope of your solution set —
and that should get to the right answer.
